0..72 Comment "// A recursion handed through a higher-order function: `apply` takes its"
73..148 Comment "// function and its argument at one type parameter, and a call instantiates"
149..225 Comment "// that parameter at the argument\'s stage (#393). A recursion\'s claim handed"
226..305 Comment "// on beside its predecessor takes the predecessor at that stage, one below its"
306..382 Comment "// own. `count`\'s run waits on the machine taking a type code as an argument"
383..460 Comment "// (#452). `applyAt` is quantified over a stage instead of a type, and a call"
461..538 Comment "// instantiates the stage from its arguments (#392); `predAt` states that its"
539..617 Comment "// result stands at its argument\'s stage, which a definition with no recursion"
618..697 Comment "// otherwise opens none for; `sameAt` takes both arguments at the stage itself,"
698..775 Comment "// which a call solves from the bound each argument shows (#1227); a call may"
776..851 Comment "// supply a stage by its binder\'s name, `applyAt[i = j]`, which must be the"
852..907 Comment "// stage the arguments show (#1227). What refuses is in"
908..962 Comment "// `../../pathological/data/sized-higher-order.gandr`."
964..1006 Comment "// The naturals the recursion takes apart."
